Feature: cat
  cat prints the target state of files, scripts and symlinks: what apply would write.

  # Background: the txtar script's mksourcedir, without the encrypted, readonly, remove and
  # template entries, plus a script.
  Background:
    Given an empty home directory
    And the source state manages:
      | target             | kind       | contents                       |
      | ~/.create          | create     | # contents of .create          |
      | ~/.dir             | directory  |                                |
      | ~/.dir/file        | file       | # contents of .dir/file        |
      | ~/.dir/subdir      | exact      |                                |
      | ~/.dir/subdir/file | file       | # contents of .dir/subdir/file |
      | ~/.empty           | empty      |                                |
      | ~/.executable      | executable | # contents of .executable      |
      | ~/.file            | file       | # contents of .file            |
      | ~/.private         | private    | # contents of .private         |
      | ~/.symlink         | symlink    | .dir/subdir/file               |
      | ~/script           | script     | #!/bin/sh                      |

  # setup (from txtar):
  #   mkhomedir golden
  #   mksourcedir

  Scenario: cat prints an empty file
    When I run "cat ~/.empty"
    Then it succeeds
    And the output is empty

  Scenario: cat prints a file
    When I run "cat ~/.file"
    Then it succeeds
    And the output is:
      """
      # contents of .file
      """

  Scenario: cat prints a symlink
    When I run "cat ~/.symlink"
    Then it succeeds
    And the output is:
      """
      .dir/subdir/file
      """

